<div class="section-label">Property Owner Impact</div>
<h2>Uncertainty for Phase 4 Area Properties</h2>

<p>If your property is south of Box Elder County line (north of 4000 South), you may be in the long-range planning corridor, but:</p>

<ul style="margin: 16px 0 20px 24px; line-height: 2.2;">
  <li><strong>No alignment has been identified.</strong> The corridor could shift east or west; your property may or may not be affected.</li>
  <li><strong>No impact study has been conducted.</strong> Wetlands, agricultural value, residential impacts, and environmental effects are unknown.</li>
  <li><strong>Corridor Preservation has not begun.</strong> UDOT has made no advance acquisitions for Phase 4 (unlike Phase 3).</li>
  <li><strong>Timeline is 17–24+ years away.</strong> Your options and circumstances will change dramatically over that timeframe.</li>
  <li><strong>Uncertainty persists for decades.</strong> Unlike Phase 3 owners (who will see clarity in Fall 2026), Phase 4 owners face prolonged ambiguity.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout warning">
  <p><strong>For Phase 4 property owners:</strong> There is no immediate action needed. Focus on property management and life planning. When Phase 4 enters formal study (est. 2043+), an State Environmental Study will be initiated and alignment maps will be published. That will be your first opportunity for certainty. Until then, assume long-term uncertainty.</p>
</div>

<div class="section-label">Broader Wasatch Front Context</div>
<h2>SR-177's Role in Regional Transportation</h2>

<p>SR-177 is one component of the broader Wasatch Front transportation strategy, as outlined in the Wasatch Front Regional Council 2023–2050 Regional Transportation Plan. Other major initiatives that may compete for funding with Phase 3 and 4:</p>

<ul style="margin: 16px 0 20px 24px; line-height: 2.2;">
  <li><strong>I-15 Widening:</strong> Continued expansion of Interstate 15 (highest priority for commuter corridor)</li>
  <li><strong>Transit Expansion:</strong> UTA bus rapid transit, light rail, commuter rail programs</li>
  <li><strong>Other Regional Corridors:</strong> US-89, UT-92, regional arterials</li>
  <li><strong>Local Jurisdiction Projects:</strong> City and county streets, utility infrastructure</li>
</ul>

<p>Phase 3 and 4 funding will depend on how UDOT and the Legislature prioritize SR-177 against these competing projects. Phase 4, being further from funded construction, will be evaluated against more recent regional priorities and population data.</p>

